pub fn title -> String = "Examples"
pub fn summary -> String =
  "Try short, runnable programs for shipping totals, record updates, and connecting functions."
pub fn heading -> String = "Choose one example and change its result"
pub fn body -> String =
  "These examples assume familiar TypeScript variables, functions, and objects. Each Open in Playground link loads the exact source shown in its panel. To run locally, complete the installation guide, then save just one example as main.ssrg in a directory outside the repository."
pub fn runCaption -> String = "Check and run the saved main.ssrg"
pub fn shippingTitle -> String = "Start here: add shipping to an order total"
pub fn shippingTask -> String =
  "Shipping is free for a subtotal of ¥5,000 or more; otherwise add ¥500. Start with familiar integer amounts, functions, and if conditions. The example inputs are subtotals of ¥3,200 and ¥5,000."
pub fn shippingReading -> String =
  "totalWithShipping accepts three integers in order: the free-shipping threshold, the fee, and the subtotal. It returns the total. let standardTotal = totalWithShipping 5000 500 fixes the first two inputs and creates a function waiting for the subtotal. standardTotal 3200 and standardTotal 5000 calculate the two orders."
pub fn shippingResult -> String =
  "The totals are ¥3,700 and ¥5,000. A subtotal exactly at ¥5,000 also receives free shipping. Change the first subtotal to 4999 and that order becomes 5499."
pub fn shippingLimit -> String =
  "Amounts in this example are whole yen. This does not promise the same decimal-number behavior as TypeScript number. Start with integer inputs from 0 to 1,000,000. The introduction compares the same task in TypeScript and explains the numeric limits."
pub fn recordsTitle -> String = "After the basics: update a score and keep the original"
pub fn recordsTask -> String =
  "Group a name and a score in a record: data whose values can be read by field name, like a familiar TypeScript object. Here, start with Aki and score 10, then create a new value with score 42."
pub fn recordsReading -> String =
  "original.score reads the old score. { ...original, score: 42 } makes a new record containing the old fields and sets its score to 42. nameOf needs only a String field named name, so updated can also contain score."
pub fn recordsEffect -> String =
  "pub effect fn main is the entry point. println prints one template string: ${...} inserts each value, and \\n separates the three output lines."
pub fn recordsResult -> String =
  "The output is 10, 42, and Aki. original has not changed. Change the new score to 99 and only the second line becomes 99. Use the new record rather than assigning into the old one."
pub fn pipelinesTitle -> String =
  "Once functions are familiar: read one calculation three ways"
pub fn pipelinesTask -> String =
  "Double 3 and add 1 to get 7. First understand ordinary calls such as double 3 and addOne 6, then compare the notation for connecting these operations."
pub fn pipelinesReading -> String =
  "3 |> double |> addOne passes each result to the next function. addOne (double 3) passes the result inside the parentheses. In addOne $ double 3, $ groups the right-hand double 3 as the argument. All three forms in this example produce the same result. do and _ <- print the three results in order."
pub fn pipelinesResult -> String =
  "The program prints three lines containing 7. To compare an input of 4, replace 3 in all three expressions with 4; it then prints three lines containing 9. Follow the inputs passed to each function even when the notation changes."
pub fn outputCaption -> String = "Expected output"
pub fn sourceLabel -> String = "Source for this example"
pub fn firstRunLabel -> String = "Run your first program"
pub fn introLabel -> String = "What Seseragi is"
pub fn functionsLabel -> String = "Function application"
pub fn recordsLabel -> String = "Records"
pub fn pipelinesLabel -> String = "Pipelines and low-precedence application"
pub fn moreTitle -> String = "Move on to web apps and larger examples"
pub fn moreBody -> String =
  "The examples above are single files that print to the console. Web apps also introduce multi-file projects, screen state, and browser events. Once your first program runs, use the dedicated walkthrough for that next step."
pub fn webLabel -> String = "Create a local web app"
pub fn catalogLabel -> String = "Official samples by task"
pub fn verification -> String =
  "The three displayed sources are checked with CLI typechecking/execution and the Playground compiler/runtime. Their links embed the same source. These checks are separate from display and interaction checks in an actual browser."
